ight)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-3\\,i-8\\,j \\\\ F(1,- \\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((-1)(1)(- \\frac{1}{2})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((-1)(1)(\\frac{1}{2})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/72/72186906f29a435fb3dbb9f09d133a2343d6baea068f2ed543147e0edaf66759.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b187716-5c52-5154-8eba-92ea2595ebca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a69a9afddf3271e21e7b0a4136a84d4420238af354869085c58d931ac2e045fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518799} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c20b29-76d4-5e38-8c58-494a801735b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac7b6358e4a38abf76e49ca6e45dd3f170dd0565e4f303fd0ba4404c277ed2ab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518828} {:answer/id #uuid "f7236076-70c6-51ad-8f1c-70ee14c70552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6f5ce40a52995b6b4cda770566885cfbfdc3f74cfade2d37f2bc88f6e55c6e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518829} {:answer/id #uuid "f98bd55b-3400-51be-83c0-e4bec78eed02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/91/91f6b076c94322c828a689829eaab50cda96b38237e21ed6d3010ae49ef89dfc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518831} {:answer/id #uuid "fda4634f-2172-562a-acac-47e97dcdd3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d818c0db25a267f3abdda3687ac96d1bd476cae70fe1e0874c3edc15b90bf997.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518832}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b187716-5c52-5154-8eba-92ea2595ebca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a69a9afddf3271e21e7b0a4136a84d4420238af354869085c58d931ac2e045fe.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518799} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f72cb0b-a380-59f9-acdc-d02f0d8440d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a2cca722-7989-59b4-a813-89c902f8a1d8" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,- \\frac{1}{4}),(-1,\\frac{5}{4}),$ and $(1,\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,- \\frac{1}{4}) & =\\left((2)(1)(- \\frac{1}{4})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(-1,\\frac{5}{4}) & =\\left((2)(-1)(\\frac{5}{4})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-\\frac{5}{2}\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,\\frac{1}{4}) & =\\left((2)(1)(\\frac{1}{4})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/a6/a69a9afddf3271e21e7b0a4136a84d4420238af354869085c58d931ac2e045fe.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d9748db-ff33-5738-9275-47347081811f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a5/a5ee72ae329a50484d2d6c41983fbcf131500e9f52619d1c6644a63a10e9b92f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518767} {:answer/id #uuid "13ef16eb-1657-5324-8294-4449e71c4000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/9560b790b63b75ce1a0e79e0beb58e0f51693723fdc8ac0642440fd4ad973937.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518770} {:answer/id #uuid "37e994b8-8c59-5ccf-a6e4-81eeb22d9cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4e79f6a378556f500223baaa090df75b002cfd83196bd23b748278ef542b0fae.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518779} {:answer/id #uuid "890ee44e-76ba-5143-a748-348d04ac2127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2b/2b09ff0226023ff4dc3dc2f8f50f3e0a7882111a64fd4170dafba4f43082d380.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518798} {:answer/id #uuid "922e2ffe-74c1-5bcd-bceb-cbe86b8efa17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/76/76268ad669c8974c2e342d00c31d0545caa1f8d86001b4e30c4142ff7ac6df23.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890ee44e-76ba-5143-a748-348d04ac2127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2b/2b09ff0226023ff4dc3dc2f8f50f3e0a7882111a64fd4170dafba4f43082d380.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518798} :answer-field/id #uuid "48cb5210-6b6f-502e-b04b-51371c35d32a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b43bc1bc-dcf4-5470-9387-8faf0f4ffb56" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,\\frac{7}{4}),(1,- \\frac{1}{4}),$ and $(-1,\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2,\\frac{7}{4}) & =\\left((2)(-2)(\\frac{7}{4})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-2)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-7\\,i-4\\,j \\\\ F(1,- \\frac{1}{4}) & =\\left((2)(1)(- \\frac{1}{4})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-j \\\\ F(-1,\\frac{7}{4}) & =\\left((2)(-1)(\\frac{7}{4})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-1)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-\\frac{7}{2}\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/2b/2b09ff0226023ff4dc3dc2f8f50f3e0a7882111a64fd4170dafba4f43082d380.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186518818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b689a3-39ce-59dd-8039-cf17d3a57d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/987e9aabd76d28c679efb27b92a2536d103e99eb3f915e6731ec37c56286876f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518764} {:answer/id #uuid "27ad4c1e-99ab-58dd-85d9-2307786676a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc92738151b6a9f4c35708a3f911287a739b7f43366f3bb18c888ca4758ac4c2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518777} {:answer/id #uuid "56105024-507d-5779-8bfb-522422369448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2d5356db4c62ed3cd1b0fd068f5d16b8ef6a3b8750011ca37ca3657ed2eaab28.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518786} {:answer/id #uuid "94538c99-4a36-5413-b710-fcab9dfdd249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e3/e3540bee55bf4207205efc609a695d4222c7707e614c86bfa8cb50b22b524d5d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518809} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd868e0-9e5d-5768-8b1a-bb8fe5d92407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ed/ed2c4c2be8527c466b58b3c56d15559161e05dccdbcaaaf380320347f367bc47.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04b689a3-39ce-59dd-8039-cf17d3a57d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/987e9aabd76d28c679efb27b92a2536d103e99eb3f915e6731ec37c56286876f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518764} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c4be32b-ebec-539c-b41e-ace6fb5e23bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5efd26b-a36e-5669-8d57-24827ceb919a" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(1,- \\frac{1}{2}),(-1,\\frac{3}{2}),$ and $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2 x y\\,i-2 x^{2}\\,j\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1,- \\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((2)(1)(- \\frac{1}{2})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-i-2\\,j \\\\ F(-1,\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\left((2)(-1)(\\frac{3}{2})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(-1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=-3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(1,\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\left((2)(1)(\\frac{1}{2})\\right)\\,i+\\left((-2)(1)^{2}\\right)\\,j=i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/98/987e9aabd76d28c679efb27b92a2536d103e99eb3f915e6731ec37c56286876f.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab5ae73e-ff79-5c66-9c67-13be3d4cfbbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+y}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519274} {:answer/id #uuid "b91672bb-93d2-50b7-8eb9-de13911fe838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519294} {:answer/id #uuid "c97c539f-b49d-5a10-804a-fdc7d978fe94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+1}{4x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519320} {:answer/id #uuid "de6ccd7b-feeb-5a8f-8280-45a2401779e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+y}{x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519358} {:answer/id #uuid "e0409b5a-bd0f-5146-b9ba-ff2fcb1d149b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+y}{2(2x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0409b5a-bd0f-5146-b9ba-ff2fcb1d149b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+y}{2(2x+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519363} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e16ecfd-ecda-506a-a72f-57bf0aaac1ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0309d911-015b-5e4c-9346-30c2d14fb0b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-145100" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{4}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2x+y}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {[2xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [4x+2] \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+1}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2x+y}{4})}{(\\frac{2x+1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+y}{4x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+y}{2(2x+1)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ea33cc-5f23-589d-8c23-7c092cbc2f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519019} {:answer/id #uuid "2589e333-131b-51b5-ad48-bd1130f65246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519052} {:answer/id #uuid "871f5b77-fc09-5ab2-85ec-496ea72f30af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519207} {:answer/id #uuid "8ccd639c-35b2-5792-9629-fe551a25b276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519221} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b2770a-9e7a-586b-b171-0fbdfcac8124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2589e333-131b-51b5-ad48-bd1130f65246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519052} :answer-field/id #uuid "6064d30a-0a9e-55a1-8d02-c3cb038cef8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05787bc3-261f-5b82-bb66-810cc0eb28ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-145108" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+x}{4y+2}, & \\,0<x<2,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<Y\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{3}{4}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{y}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{3}{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+x)}{(4\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{1/4}^{3/4}(x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{3x}{2}]}_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[{x}^{2}+3x]_{1/4}^{3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}({(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}+3(\\frac{3}{4})-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-3(\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(\\frac{9}{16}+\\frac{9}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0949f974-6ec9-5ea8-8abf-e774af8d8181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519006} {:answer/id #uuid "492c26d0-4de7-559c-b3c6-23e5c24bcbeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{y}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519108} {:answer/id #uuid "a324c1a2-ed56-5a2e-94f6-eaae1cefbad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519261} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a0eac0-1e8a-5d29-8b9c-eb8fd34e5908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519336} {:answer/id #uuid "ea3319c5-cf98-59ef-a3ca-016ee01f17e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0949f974-6ec9-5ea8-8abf-e774af8d8181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519006} :answer-field/id #uuid "12430dc2-f5be-5736-9417-2b04f094d03d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1967c2eb-a9e1-5379-9ffd-3708e127b5c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143637" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{e}^{x+y}}{2})}{(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2})} \\\\  & ={e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032b45ef-0f8d-5228-ab53-44d951ab7b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518991} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef38244-8fdf-5af9-a467-8e572c929ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519015} {:answer/id #uuid "3f275ff8-361f-502e-8e26-7ac774e7b63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519093} {:answer/id #uuid "c3efee66-91d8-51df-9c99-66043ccf9a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.7%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519316} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a1db81-4260-5c90-a443-a427b1dbd8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.3%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b45ef-0f8d-5228-ab53-44d951ab7b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518991} :answer-field/id #uuid "d314da54-c3a8-55ac-8066-a2906576f626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "217980d1-12ff-5532-90b2-461f43899f04" :question/math-academy-id "q-95091" :question/problem "A truck travels an average distance of $21\\,\\text{mi}$ with a gallon of gas. However, $15%$ of the time, a truck travels more than $26\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Assuming that the distance the truck can travel with a gallon of gas is normally distributed, find, as a percent, the proportion of journeys that the truck travels less than $15\\,\\text{mi}$ on a gallon of gas. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0918\\,$ | $\\,0.0901\\,$ | $\\,0.0885\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ | $\\,0.1075\\,$ | $\\,0.1056\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ | $\\,0.1271\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the distance traveled by the truck with a gallon of gas. Then we have $X∼N(21,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X\\le 15).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>26)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =1-P(X>26) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 26) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{26-21}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2073 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2073} \\\\  & ≈4.8239.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\le 15)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 15) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{15-21}{4.8239}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.24) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.24) \\\\  & =0.1075 \\\\  & ≈10.8%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $10.8%$ of the time, a gallon of gas travels only $15\\,\\text{mi}$ or less."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41d76f20-9c01-572e-9bec-b4534a624512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16xy(4-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519095} {:answer/id #uuid "697349f9-9995-58d2-a52b-dd64ff7d8349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{{y}^{2}(4-y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519146} {:answer/id #uuid "9307b7d8-60d8-57f7-a4d6-b9b56f00ed28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}(4-y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519235} {:answer/id #uuid "b28cbfc4-beef-5aec-838d-bb8099b27e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519285} {:answer/id #uuid "bd07ced2-884b-510d-b5ef-173e5b1d1caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4xy}{4-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b28cbfc4-beef-5aec-838d-bb8099b27e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519285} :answer-field/id #uuid "f41b096e-82c3-5dae-bf25-98903c7a8157" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b02f20a-35d0-57b1-ada9-f1273f1f3904" :question/math-academy-id "q-143641" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32} & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}{\\int }_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{32}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{y/2}^{\\sqrt{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{64}\\cdot (y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256}, & \\,0<y<4 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5x{y}^{2}}{32})}{(\\frac{5{y}^{3}(4-y)}{256})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8x}{y(4-y)}, & \\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{y},\\,0<y<4, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b5b39e-b50f-5667-85aa-10f98316c5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+y}{3(y+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518997} {:answer/id #uuid "099b70eb-adf5-5c0b-935e-950c1e096556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{2y+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519007} {:answer/id #uuid "41e1bf01-9793-54f8-9d28-af6f78aec84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+2}{x+2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519096} {:answer/id #uuid "476db03a-613d-54d1-8c90-950c1e79d5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y+1}{2({x}^{2}+y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519104} {:answer/id #uuid "5dfec5ee-76b0-5274-a7c7-3fbca567739b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dfec5ee-76b0-5274-a7c7-3fbca567739b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519133} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb8d45a-30b0-5de1-a6e8-4ab5704badae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60bd267c-251c-5e47-b502-67f097fb7ef0" :question/math-academy-id "q-145101" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{y}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{36}+\\frac{xy}{6}]}_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{36}+\\frac{3y}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{y}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y+3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y+3}{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{12})}{(\\frac{2y+3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2y}{3(2y+3)}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3915e834-8837-5560-8ead-24299d5e8d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519080} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9b3fac-024e-544d-b2ef-d778f12b1db5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519109} {:answer/id #uuid "71b20e91-2ea6-54a7-a133-a83a08132141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519163} {:answer/id #uuid "745ed225-fd23-55f2-9adb-b6580902b88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519173} {:answer/id #uuid "931f0d20-f406-5676-b3e6-461c30b95ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71b20e91-2ea6-54a7-a133-a83a08132141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519163} :answer-field/id #uuid "91dddd11-2a4d-5015-8ccb-b2a5b340c5c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a8bad50-df40-5364-9a82-59b2638eab96" :question/math-academy-id "q-143607" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3739f1be-769b-5241-ac9d-166ae50d15a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4|x|\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519076} {:answer/id #uuid "54d6cd33-d4de-58b7-8f64-738c8128b497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519123} {:answer/id #uuid "85e270b6-45ce-567e-9271-f69abcdff687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519203} {:answer/id #uuid "d06920a3-ba34-58db-ba10-ad2913c12c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4|x|\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519332} {:answer/id #uuid "db27974c-ad6f-568e-a27d-852868230ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e270b6-45ce-567e-9271-f69abcdff687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519203} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0359eb2-a7b5-51ab-a406-e6a8025c07cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e70aada-1f44-5184-b986-16d8ac2cfc36" :question/math-academy-id "q-95503" :question/problem "$$\n\nIf $y=-3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡4x,$ then $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse hyperbolic cosecant function is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡x)=-\\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula above with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡4x) \\\\  & =-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡4x) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{|4x|\\sqrt{1+(4x)^{2}}})\\cdot (4x)^{′} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{4|x|\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{|x|\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7d0ba3-02f6-59a7-be65-bf8f3801013a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519040} {:answer/id #uuid "3da4eaa1-6cda-5d99-b813-20a4d9738e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519091} {:answer/id #uuid "58bb21c1-450d-505d-b6dc-9db3c242bf5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519125} {:answer/id #uuid "70730109-0401-592f-81a2-e4405b7b0f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519162} {:answer/id #uuid "e7050eac-3947-55c2-b302-d2bd01b29003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e7d0ba3-02f6-59a7-be65-bf8f3801013a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519040} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c446274-85cc-56ab-805a-72556fc77fd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91a2a3a1-8ae1-5b13-976f-9bc558602e26" :question/math-academy-id "q-143619" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3x, & \\,y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(X\\in A\\,|\\,Y=y)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$  \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{y}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{y}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $y<x<1\\,,\\,0<y<1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x}{(\\frac{3}{2}(1-{y}^{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{1-{y}^{2}}, & \\,0<y<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) & =P(\\frac{1}{4}<X\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{1}{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(1-{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1}\\frac{2x}{(\\frac{35}{36})}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{35}(1-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{28}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2339586b-683d-5a76-b2a4-04bd1b2acd78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519048} {:answer/id #uuid "65bb09ec-3b2d-50b3-ad37-144c0a7328e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25{x}^{2}y}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519142} {:answer/id #uuid "b5172b40-2e6a-503d-a0e7-85b7d1a5c74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519291} {:answer/id #uuid "eeed90d5-0dc1-5550-b397-1136f6576d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519385} {:answer/id #uuid "f14caf42-2818-5734-9146-91625adaf049" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5172b40-2e6a-503d-a0e7-85b7d1a5c74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519291} :answer-field/id #uuid "59042651-4b23-5e23-bf65-fd2e8cfe07fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3c1aae3-e457-548d-bfd0-11222eb5ea0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143610" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5y\\sqrt{x}, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x\\sqrt{x}}{2}, & 0<x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5y\\sqrt{x}}{(\\frac{5x\\sqrt{x}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{x}, & 0<x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x} \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09021ce8-047e-5b0d-92f8-36d32076c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519005} {:answer/id #uuid "2d58aa30-649a-5d30-86fd-836c82705dcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83.4%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519065} {:answer/id #uuid "7bce9ce0-a3ff-570e-86db-d819bc4f24bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80.8%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519187} {:answer/id #uuid "99059b85-08c0-5911-bef5-b258cf55a646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519244} {:answer/id #uuid "99eaaeec-7b23-5191-b1ac-c727b2e8bc60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09021ce8-047e-5b0d-92f8-36d32076c19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78.2%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519005} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8d7f1f-3c0d-5b85-87f8-4a2364ded62c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d34b6be0-737c-5c5c-907f-c713ce8e6b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-133236" :question/problem "The average height of a player in a particular professional basketball league is $78$ inches. Additionally, $15%$ of players are over $82$ inches tall. Assuming that a player's height is normally distributed, find the percentage of players over $75$ inches tall, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ | $\\,0.2148\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected player. Then we have $X∼N(78,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>75).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X>82)=0.15.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =1-P(X>82) \\\\  & =1-0.15 \\\\  & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 82) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{82-78}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4}{\\sigma }) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)≈1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sigma } & =1.0364 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.2591 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.2591} \\\\  & ≈3.85951.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X>75)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>75) & =1-P(X\\le 75) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{75-78}{3.85951}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le -0.78) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.78) \\\\  & =1-0.2177 \\\\  & =0.7823 \\\\  & ≈78.2%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $78.2%$ of players are over $75$ inch tall."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f88efb-26a7-5927-a3a0-854d67c40077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f88efb-26a7-5927-a3a0-854d67c40077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3121" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519199} :answer-field/id #uuid "9151a6ca-f2c2-5987-ba7e-6def5ae3cbbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6b501ec-18d5-51e8-9a88-2c1c73e44bb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-324738" :question/problem "The lifespan of LED light bulbs is normally distributed with a mean of $40,000$ hours and an unknown standard deviation $\\sigma .$ Tests show that $10%$ of bulbs fail before $36,000$ hours. Assuming a normal model, find the standard deviation, **rounded to the nearest integer.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $\\,z\\,$ | $0.75$ | $0.8$ | $0.85$ | $0.9$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$h$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the lifespan of an LED bulb. Then we have $X∼N(40,000,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<36,000)=0.10.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<36,000) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{36,000-40,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma }) & =0.10 \\\\ -\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.9)=1.2816,\n$$\n\nand therefore, by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)=-1.2816.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =-1.2816 \\\\ \\frac{4,000}{\\sigma } & =1.2816 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4,000}{1.2816} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈3,121\\,\\text{h.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf6bb84-99f4-501b-898f-55beecfd0ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.89" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519011} {:answer/id #uuid "6e877167-064e-5961-ba0d-9292251730e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519157} {:answer/id #uuid "a07531b8-8752-5b56-b8df-4b8c0357540d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519253} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0bfe5f-9010-5b76-900e-a0a88d617f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519356} {:answer/id #uuid "f64b54b4-f561-592c-ab77-9cb0208b121a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc0bfe5f-9010-5b76-900e-a0a88d617f3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519356} :answer-field/id #uuid "c153adbb-ebdd-563f-aa07-ecead9219cce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e64f0682-cd52-5135-b0b3-08e76e7a1873" :question/math-academy-id "q-95082" :question/problem "A group of students took a test and got an average score of $79.$ Also, $15%$ of the students scored lower than $66.$ Assuming that the scores of the students are normally distributed, find the standard deviation of their scores, rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the grade obtained by a student. Then we have $X∼N(79,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 66)=0.15.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 66) & =0.15 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{66-79}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{13}{\\sigma }) & =0.15 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364,\n$$\n\nand therefore by symmetry, we have\n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(1-0.85)={Φ}^{-1}(0.15)=-1.0364.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{\\sigma } & =-1.0364 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{-13}{-1.0364} \\\\  & ≈12.54.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519592 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0892c552-097f-5f93-9810-e84e7169c037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519004} {:answer/id #uuid "523d9b76-cebc-5f99-9fbb-0e7e6db546ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519118} {:answer/id #uuid "54b15bb6-9e55-5f2e-9988-527ed5cf6ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519122} {:answer/id #uuid "653110f4-0f51-5e63-bd19-c507464feb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+{y}^{2}}{9x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519140} {:answer/id #uuid "726600a2-7334-5b46-8aea-ffaea3283afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "523d9b76-cebc-5f99-9fbb-0e7e6db546ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519118} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe76e9c1-efee-513d-8f78-b81b7c1d3e0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6d9b032-eda8-55d5-b69c-37b94f5fdf98" :question/math-academy-id "q-144941" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9}{10}, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{10})}{(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+9}{10})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}+9}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
